WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.490
Welcome.

00:00:00.892 --> 00:00:03.919
This course will take you from the three files
you started with

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:06.996
to a clear explanation of the repository you now
have.

00:00:07.398 --> 00:00:10.161
You do not need a networking or machine learning
background.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:14.381
We will read the paper&#x27;s ideas, inspect both
spreadsheet shaped

00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:18.865
datasets, follow a flow through the original
model, and examine

00:00:18.865 --> 00:00:20.686
the experiments that actually ran.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.069
Then we will walk through the code, setup,
tests, downloads,

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.518
demonstration, and hardware roadmap.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.143
Codex wrote and produced this lesson.

00:00:30.545 --> 00:00:33.044
A synthetic voice renders the narration.

00:00:33.446 --> 00:00:36.159
The research claims come from recorded evidence.

00:00:36.634 --> 00:00:40.589
The central result is a working, measured
reproduction attempt.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:44.732
The original multimodal classifier trained and
performed

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:49.440
inference on compatible research flows using an
NVIDIA G B ten

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.608
graphics processor.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.504
Three full fine tuning runs achieved an average
test accuracy of

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:57.438
eighty six point six five percent.

00:00:57.839 --> 00:01:01.769
The paper reports ninety seven point five
percent for its

00:01:01.769 --> 00:01:02.384
comparison.

00:01:02.786 --> 00:01:04.418
We did not reproduce that number.

00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:08.486
We also did not build a complete live intrusion
detection system.

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:11.311
Those limits do not erase the work.

00:01:11.725 --> 00:01:15.128
They tell you precisely what the work
establishes and what a next

00:01:15.128 --> 00:01:16.823
experiment needs to resolve.

00:01:17.275 --> 00:01:19.686
A network moves information in packets.

00:01:20.126 --> 00:01:22.688
Think of one packet as a small delivery
envelope.

00:01:23.077 --> 00:01:26.204
Its header contains information used to handle
the delivery.

00:01:26.618 --> 00:01:30.373
Its payload contains the carried content, which
may be encrypted.

00:01:30.775 --> 00:01:34.617
A byte is a small numerical unit with values
from zero to two

00:01:34.617 --> 00:01:35.546
hundred fifty five.

00:01:35.948 --> 00:01:39.326
The model can process those numbers without
turning them into

00:01:39.339 --> 00:01:40.406
readable sentences.

00:01:40.808 --> 00:01:45.128
The envelope analogy is only a starting point:
real packets use

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:49.046
several protocol layers, and a payload is not
guaranteed to

00:01:49.058 --> 00:01:51.193
contain a complete application message.

00:01:51.607 --> 00:01:54.998
One conversation usually spans many packets.

00:01:55.455 --> 00:01:59.409
A flow is a set of related packets, grouped
using an explicit

00:01:59.409 --> 00:01:59.773
rule.

00:02:00.187 --> 00:02:03.916
A common starting point uses source and
destination addresses,

00:02:04.179 --> 00:02:07.280
source and destination ports, and the transport
protocol.

00:02:07.669 --> 00:02:11.360
Direction handling, timeouts, and packet
ordering also need a

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:11.975
definition.

00:02:12.364 --> 00:02:15.628
Imagine two cameras sending messages at the same
time.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.633
The next two captured packets could belong to
different cameras.

00:02:20.034 --> 00:02:24.014
Their proximity in a file does not make them one
conversation.

00:02:24.428 --> 00:02:28.144
This matters because Net Mamba Plus learns from
a structured view

00:02:28.144 --> 00:02:28.784
of a flow.

00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:33.743
Supplying unrelated packet rows changes the
meaning of its input.

00:02:34.175 --> 00:02:39.200
An intrusion detection system, often shortened
to I D S, observes

00:02:39.213 --> 00:02:41.788
traffic and raises alerts for investigation.

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:45.444
The neural classifier is one component that can
help assign a

00:02:45.456 --> 00:02:46.047
category.

00:02:46.461 --> 00:02:51.310
Capture, flow assembly, data transformation,
alert thresholds,

00:02:51.561 --> 00:02:54.828
event storage, and operational response are
additional

00:02:54.828 --> 00:02:55.519
components.

00:02:55.895 --> 00:02:59.224
A prediction alone does not decide whether to
block a customer

00:02:59.237 --> 00:02:59.752
connection.

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:04.023
In this project the browser demonstration
displays previously

00:03:04.036 --> 00:03:05.229
recorded predictions.

00:03:05.644 --> 00:03:08.973
That is useful for explaining behavior and
mistakes, but opening

00:03:08.973 --> 00:03:13.093
the page does not connect to a network tap or
execute a model

00:03:13.093 --> 00:03:14.136
inside your browser.

00:03:14.651 --> 00:03:17.714
Throughout the lesson, separate three claims.

00:03:18.103 --> 00:03:21.167
Implemented means there is code intended to do a
job.

00:03:21.581 --> 00:03:24.694
Executed means a recorded command actually ran.

00:03:25.109 --> 00:03:28.473
Validated means a specific check passed against
a stated

00:03:28.486 --> 00:03:29.277
expectation.

00:03:29.666 --> 00:03:32.767
A script can exist without its hardware path
being tested.

00:03:33.156 --> 00:03:36.584
A training job can complete without matching a
published

00:03:36.596 --> 00:03:37.262
accuracy.

00:03:37.651 --> 00:03:41.442
A checksum can match without proving the dataset
is unbiased.

00:03:41.806 --> 00:03:45.912
When someone says everything works, ask which
activity, on which

00:03:45.912 --> 00:03:47.707
environment, with which evidence.

00:03:48.109 --> 00:03:52.729
The repository&#x27;s manifests, logs, predictions,
and failure

00:03:52.729 --> 00:03:54.524
records make that question answerable.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.864
You now have the vocabulary needed for the rest
of the course.

00:03:58.279 --> 00:04:01.959
We will use short questions to check
understanding, with clearly

00:04:01.959 --> 00:04:04.496
labeled pauses that resume automatically.

00:04:04.923 --> 00:04:08.578
The transcript repeats the narration and links
each scene to

00:04:08.578 --> 00:04:09.670
supporting files.

00:04:10.072 --> 00:04:14.355
The companion file guide explains every tracked
file and every

00:04:14.355 --> 00:04:17.533
release download, so you can look up a
particular filename

00:04:17.621 --> 00:04:19.467
without memorizing the directory tree.

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:22.532
First, practice the most important boundary.

00:04:22.934 --> 00:04:26.563
Does a working classifier prove that a complete
live security

00:04:26.576 --> 00:04:27.669
system has been deployed?

00:04:28.058 --> 00:04:30.620
Take ten seconds to formulate your answer.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:41.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Does a working
classifier prove a complete live IDS?

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.878
The answer is no: the classifier is one tested
component.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.601
Now open the attached paper conceptually.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:52.082
It is the January twenty twenty six first
preprint version of Net

00:04:52.094 --> 00:04:56.374
Mamba Plus, a framework of pretrained models for
efficient and

00:04:56.386 --> 00:04:58.419
accurate network traffic classification.

00:04:58.834 --> 00:05:02.297
A research paper describes a problem, a proposed
method,

00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:04.494
experiments, and conclusions.

00:05:04.883 --> 00:05:08.409
It is not an installation manual or a complete
specification of

00:05:08.547 --> 00:05:09.903
every released data file.

00:05:10.304 --> 00:05:13.781
This paper covers several model variants and
datasets.

00:05:14.157 --> 00:05:18.901
Our repository exercises a particular multimodal
Mamba classifier

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:23.494
and one compatible downstream dataset, so its
scope is narrower.

00:05:23.967 --> 00:05:26.865
The authors identify three difficulties.

00:05:27.304 --> 00:05:31.620
First, some sequence models become expensive
when processing many

00:05:31.682 --> 00:05:32.636
input units.

00:05:33.037 --> 00:05:37.729
Second, a traffic representation may discard
useful content while

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:41.568
keeping misleading clues, such as identifiers
tied to the

00:05:41.568 --> 00:05:42.596
collection environment.

00:05:43.061 --> 00:05:46.887
Third, common categories can dominate learning
while rare

00:05:46.887 --> 00:05:48.480
categories perform poorly.

00:05:48.894 --> 00:05:50.249
These are distinct problems.

00:05:50.638 --> 00:05:54.778
A faster model does not automatically fix biased
input data.

00:05:55.192 --> 00:05:59.307
Adding packet timing does not automatically
solve a rare class

00:05:59.307 --> 00:05:59.771
problem.

00:06:00.197 --> 00:06:04.187
The paper combines architectural,
representation, and training

00:06:04.187 --> 00:06:05.642
ideas to address them.

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:09.318
Our measurements should be interpreted against
the parts actually

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:09.870
enabled.

00:06:10.334 --> 00:06:14.059
Multimodal means using several views of the same
observation.

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:18.550
Here it means packet byte content, packet
lengths, and time gaps.

00:06:18.952 --> 00:06:23.104
Suppose two flows contain similarly encrypted
bytes, but one

00:06:23.104 --> 00:06:26.830
sends a steady series of small packets while
another sends large

00:06:26.842 --> 00:06:27.419
bursts.

00:06:27.833 --> 00:06:31.020
Size and timing can provide additional
structure.

00:06:31.433 --> 00:06:35.046
This is an explanatory example, not a measured
claim about our

00:06:35.059 --> 00:06:35.811
test set.

00:06:36.225 --> 00:06:39.838
The important requirement is that all three
views describe the

00:06:39.838 --> 00:06:40.716
same flow.

00:06:41.105 --> 00:06:44.041
Combining bytes from one connection with timing
from another

00:06:44.041 --> 00:06:48.030
would create a physically inconsistent example
even if the tensor

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:49.385
dimensions still matched.

00:06:49.846 --> 00:06:53.447
The paper discusses Mamba and an alternative
optimized

00:06:53.447 --> 00:06:55.342
Transformer called Net Trans.

00:06:55.442 --> 00:06:58.441
Flash Attention belongs to that alternative&#x27;s
attention

00:06:58.441 --> 00:07:01.390
implementation; it is not evidence that our
active Mamba

00:07:01.402 --> 00:07:03.585
classifier uses an attention layer.

00:07:03.962 --> 00:07:07.299
A Mamba block processes a sequence while
updating numerical

00:07:07.299 --> 00:07:10.298
state, with updates influenced by the input.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:12.895
This can make sequence processing efficient.

00:07:13.297 --> 00:07:17.199
It does not mean the software maintains a live
table of network

00:07:17.212 --> 00:07:18.905
connections between predictions.

00:07:19.307 --> 00:07:23.184
In our tested configuration the classifier uses
four Mamba

00:07:23.184 --> 00:07:23.761
blocks.

00:07:24.163 --> 00:07:27.337
Other branches present in a source repository
are not

00:07:27.425 --> 00:07:30.185
automatically exercised by that configuration.

00:07:30.705 --> 00:07:34.119
For a first reading, use the framework diagram
to understand the

00:07:34.132 --> 00:07:35.136
overall path.

00:07:35.537 --> 00:07:38.889
Then read the traffic representation section to
see how raw

00:07:38.889 --> 00:07:41.224
traffic becomes structured inputs.

00:07:41.638 --> 00:07:45.190
The model details explain embeddings, sequence
processing,

00:07:45.454 --> 00:07:47.462
pretraining, and classification.

00:07:47.864 --> 00:07:51.718
The online system section adds capture and
shared memory around a

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:52.534
classifier.

00:07:52.935 --> 00:07:57.128
The evaluation section reports dataset
comparisons, efficiency,

00:07:57.379 --> 00:07:58.747
and other experiments.

00:07:59.161 --> 00:08:03.705
Keep a note beside each number identifying the
model, task, and

00:08:03.705 --> 00:08:04.885
machine that produced it.

00:08:05.299 --> 00:08:08.551
This reading habit prevents a result from one
experiment being

00:08:08.626 --> 00:08:11.977
accidentally used as proof for a different
system.

00:08:12.446 --> 00:08:16.325
The paper&#x27;s comparison includes a ninety seven
point five percent

00:08:16.438 --> 00:08:21.898
accuracy figure for the relevant C I C IoT
twenty twenty two

00:08:21.898 --> 00:08:22.350
result.

00:08:22.752 --> 00:08:26.266
Our three seed mean is eighty six point six five
percent.

00:08:26.668 --> 00:08:30.484
We preserve both numbers and the differences in
runtime and

00:08:30.484 --> 00:08:31.488
training settings.

00:08:31.877 --> 00:08:34.124
We did not isolate the cause of the gap.

00:08:34.526 --> 00:08:38.266
The paper also studies few shot learning,
unfamiliar

00:08:38.266 --> 00:08:41.869
distributions, and a label distribution aware
strategy for

00:08:41.881 --> 00:08:43.011
imbalanced classes.

00:08:43.425 --> 00:08:47.555
Those results do not become reproduced merely
because our basic

00:08:47.555 --> 00:08:48.835
classifier runs.

00:08:49.249 --> 00:08:52.350
Each additional claim would need its own
matching protocol,

00:08:52.777 --> 00:08:54.597
inputs, and measured outputs.

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:57.325
Two details deserve special care.

00:08:57.727 --> 00:09:01.616
A token is a numerical input unit for the
sequence model; we will

00:09:01.629 --> 00:09:02.971
build those units later.

00:09:03.398 --> 00:09:07.275
The paper&#x27;s hyperparameter table shows four
hundred one tokens

00:09:07.375 --> 00:09:09.031
for its byte representation.

00:09:09.433 --> 00:09:13.548
Our actual multimodal classifier receives four
hundred forty

00:09:13.548 --> 00:09:17.989
three tokens, after including sizes, intervals,
and three summary

00:09:17.989 --> 00:09:18.592
tokens.

00:09:18.993 --> 00:09:23.209
Later, the paper reports online throughput of
two hundred sixty

00:09:23.221 --> 00:09:27.186
one point eight seven megabits per second for
its Net Mamba

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:28.892
prototype on different hardware.

00:09:29.294 --> 00:09:31.916
That is not our measured multimodal deployment.

00:09:32.318 --> 00:09:36.546
Before presenting any paper number, identify the
exact experiment

00:09:36.546 --> 00:09:37.411
it describes.

00:09:37.825 --> 00:09:42.769
Take ten seconds to name one paper claim that
this repository has

00:09:42.769 --> 00:09:44.638
not independently reproduced.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:55.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Name one paper
claim that has not been reproduced here.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:58.525
Examples include the paper&#x27;s headline accuracy,
its full

00:09:58.538 --> 00:10:01.686
pretraining protocol, and its online throughput.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.835
Next, inspect your first attached data file.

00:10:05.237 --> 00:10:09.652
A C S V is plain text organized as rows and
comma separated

00:10:09.665 --> 00:10:10.280
fields.

00:10:10.669 --> 00:10:13.980
A spreadsheet can display it as a table, but
this file is not a

00:10:13.993 --> 00:10:16.540
database server or a network capture program.

00:10:16.941 --> 00:10:20.466
The full profile counted one million four
hundred ten thousand

00:10:20.629 --> 00:10:25.183
two hundred fifty five packet records in the C I
C I D S

00:10:25.371 --> 00:10:26.688
twenty seventeen file.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.247
Each row has fifteen hundred five columns.

00:10:29.661 --> 00:10:32.873
The stored profile lets you inspect that
structure without

00:10:32.886 --> 00:10:35.269
opening a multi gigabyte spreadsheet.

00:10:35.784 --> 00:10:39.121
Fifteen hundred columns are named payload byte
one through

00:10:39.121 --> 00:10:42.834
payload byte fifteen hundred, using underscores
in the exact

00:10:42.834 --> 00:10:43.536
header names.

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:46.459
They describe payload byte positions.

00:10:46.911 --> 00:10:51.125
Five additional columns are time to live, total
length, protocol,

00:10:51.401 --> 00:10:53.521
a delta time field, and the label.

00:10:53.923 --> 00:10:57.949
Time to live is a protocol field limiting how
far a packet can

00:10:57.962 --> 00:10:59.969
travel; it is not a timestamp.

00:11:00.383 --> 00:11:03.042
Total length is a recorded packet length value.

00:11:03.444 --> 00:11:06.153
Protocol describes the communication protocol.

00:11:06.567 --> 00:11:10.983
Delta time suggests a time difference, but the
CSV alone does not

00:11:10.983 --> 00:11:13.567
establish its units or how it was computed.

00:11:13.981 --> 00:11:16.490
The label is the category supplied with the
record.

00:11:16.967 --> 00:11:19.977
This CSV contains fifteen label names.

00:11:20.378 --> 00:11:24.555
The largest group is labeled benign, with three
hundred sixty two

00:11:24.705 --> 00:11:26.536
thousand one hundred eight rows.

00:11:26.938 --> 00:11:30.901
Do S Hulk has two hundred fifty thousand, and D
Do S has two

00:11:30.901 --> 00:11:33.183
hundred forty one thousand four hundred five.

00:11:33.585 --> 00:11:37.297
In general terms, a denial of service attack
attempts to disrupt

00:11:37.297 --> 00:11:41.173
availability, and a distributed denial of
service uses multiple

00:11:41.173 --> 00:11:41.850
sources.

00:11:42.289 --> 00:11:45.813
Here those are dataset labels, not events newly
detected by our

00:11:45.813 --> 00:11:46.252
model.

00:11:46.678 --> 00:11:50.855
The table also contains credential attack, web
attack, scanning,

00:11:51.106 --> 00:11:54.141
bot, infiltration, and other categories.

00:11:54.542 --> 00:11:59.446
Their counts describe this supplied extract, not
every version of

00:11:59.446 --> 00:12:00.562
the original dataset.

00:12:01.034 --> 00:12:04.596
At the small end, Port Scan has eight hundred
thirty rows.

00:12:05.022 --> 00:12:08.208
The web attack SQL injection label has only
twelve.

00:12:08.622 --> 00:12:11.857
That is why a single overall accuracy can hide a
serious

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:12.485
weakness.

00:12:12.873 --> 00:12:16.887
Imagine a model that handles a common label well
but fails every

00:12:16.899 --> 00:12:17.727
rare example.

00:12:18.116 --> 00:12:20.725
Its aggregate score might still look impressive.

00:12:21.101 --> 00:12:24.613
A defensible packet classifier study would
examine per class

00:12:24.625 --> 00:12:28.965
support, precision, recall, and a confusion
matrix, with careful

00:12:28.965 --> 00:12:29.404
splitting.

00:12:29.805 --> 00:12:32.314
We have not trained that packet classifier here.

00:12:32.715 --> 00:12:36.026
These counts explain a possible future
adaptation and its

00:12:36.039 --> 00:12:39.488
evaluation requirements; they do not report new
model

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:40.290
performance.

00:12:40.767 --> 00:12:45.383
The full metadata scan also found negative delta
times, with a

00:12:45.396 --> 00:12:49.874
minimum of negative zero point zero zero zero
zero one eight.

00:12:50.275 --> 00:12:52.308
We do not know the time unit from this extract.

00:12:52.721 --> 00:12:56.899
Some recorded total lengths exceed fifteen
hundred even though

00:12:56.911 --> 00:12:58.793
there are fifteen hundred payload columns.

00:12:59.194 --> 00:13:02.581
Those observations are reasons to investigate
the extraction

00:13:02.581 --> 00:13:03.271
procedure.

00:13:03.685 --> 00:13:07.348
They are not enough to diagnose corruption,
reconstruct missing

00:13:07.348 --> 00:13:09.430
headers, or invent time ordering.

00:13:09.819 --> 00:13:13.269
A field name is a clue, not a complete data
contract.

00:13:13.657 --> 00:13:17.258
Our strict native flow path requires observed
intervals to be

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:21.397
finite and nonnegative, so blindly copying these
values would be

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:22.827
unsafe scientifically.

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:27.565
The profiling program scans the full file to
count rows and

00:13:27.577 --> 00:13:29.484
aggregate the selected metadata.

00:13:29.897 --> 00:13:33.321
That is stronger evidence than looking at the
first fifty rows.

00:13:33.748 --> 00:13:37.536
However, it is narrower than exhaustively
validating every

00:13:37.548 --> 00:13:38.802
payload byte cell.

00:13:39.216 --> 00:13:42.063
The report states that limit explicitly.

00:13:42.452 --> 00:13:46.729
It records file size and a content hash so
another person can

00:13:46.729 --> 00:13:48.209
identify the same input.

00:13:48.623 --> 00:13:52.699
It does not recover capture history, packet
direction, or a flow

00:13:52.699 --> 00:13:55.019
identifier that the columns do not supply.

00:13:55.408 --> 00:13:59.697
Keeping the exact scope of a data audit visible
prevents a useful

00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:03.008
profile from being mistaken for a complete
certification.

00:14:03.454 --> 00:14:07.292
Most importantly, the supplied schema does not
establish source

00:14:07.292 --> 00:14:11.794
and destination addresses, ports, a flow
identifier, or packet

00:14:11.794 --> 00:14:12.760
order within a flow.

00:14:13.161 --> 00:14:17.124
Its payload byte columns also do not establish
the paper&#x27;s header

00:14:17.413 --> 00:14:18.817
and payload representation.

00:14:19.218 --> 00:14:23.420
Taking five neighboring rows and calling them
one flow would add

00:14:23.432 --> 00:14:24.724
an unsupported assumption.

00:14:25.125 --> 00:14:28.988
A classifier trained directly on these rows
could still be an

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:32.336
interesting project, but it would be a packet
based adaptation

00:14:32.349 --> 00:14:34.355
with its own labels and evaluation.

00:14:34.757 --> 00:14:38.331
Take ten seconds: explain why matching the
number of numeric

00:14:38.343 --> 00:14:41.993
columns would not make these rows compatible Net
Mamba Plus

00:14:42.005 --> 00:14:42.632
inputs.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:53.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Why is matching a
tensor shape insufficient to establish
compatible flow data?

00:14:53.088 --> 00:14:57.288
A tensor shape describes dimensions, not the
meaning or origin of

00:14:57.288 --> 00:14:57.939
the numbers.

00:14:58.339 --> 00:15:01.214
Your second CSV makes that distinction clearer.

00:15:01.627 --> 00:15:06.353
The U N S W extract contains seventy nine
thousand eight hundred

00:15:06.503 --> 00:15:07.565
eighty one rows.

00:15:07.966 --> 00:15:11.516
It has the same broad structure: fifteen hundred
payload byte

00:15:11.516 --> 00:15:15.905
columns followed by time to live, total length,
protocol, delta

00:15:15.905 --> 00:15:17.055
time, and label.

00:15:17.467 --> 00:15:19.193
There are ten category names.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:22.806
The common column layout makes joint profiling
convenient.

00:15:23.219 --> 00:15:26.969
It does not prove the two files were captured,
labeled, or

00:15:26.982 --> 00:15:30.133
extracted with the same procedure, and it does
not make their

00:15:30.145 --> 00:15:31.820
category names interchangeable.

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:37.116
The largest U N S W category is normal, with
twenty one thousand

00:15:37.116 --> 00:15:37.616
rows.

00:15:38.029 --> 00:15:42.069
Generic contains seventeen thousand five hundred
eighty, and

00:15:42.082 --> 00:15:45.872
exploits contains thirteen thousand nine hundred
ninety two.

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:49.637
Fuzzers and reconnaissance are also substantial
groups.

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:53.390
At a high level, reconnaissance means attempts
to learn about a

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:57.943
target, and fuzzing probes how software handles
unusual inputs.

00:15:58.355 --> 00:16:01.908
These descriptions help interpret the names, but
the exact

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:04.785
labeling rules come from the dataset&#x27;s
production process.

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:07.287
The table is a count of stored labels.

00:16:07.687 --> 00:16:11.252
It is not a claim that our trained six class
model recognizes

00:16:11.264 --> 00:16:12.728
these ten categories.

00:16:13.213 --> 00:16:18.266
The remaining labels are do s, backdoor,
analysis, shellcode, and

00:16:18.266 --> 00:16:18.828
worms.

00:16:19.229 --> 00:16:21.380
Worms has only ninety three rows.

00:16:21.768 --> 00:16:25.807
Unlike the mostly T C P and U D P protocol
entries in the

00:16:25.807 --> 00:16:29.672
first extract, this file includes a broader
range of protocol

00:16:29.685 --> 00:16:30.248
strings.

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:34.000
Some may be uncommon to a beginner, but the
practical lesson is

00:16:34.012 --> 00:16:38.227
simple: a future preprocessing program must
handle the actual

00:16:38.227 --> 00:16:41.129
declared values rather than assume two
protocols.

00:16:41.529 --> 00:16:44.131
The full profile records their counts by label.

00:16:44.531 --> 00:16:48.896
Again, no packet model was trained on this
extract, and these

00:16:48.896 --> 00:16:52.723
category frequencies do not describe the native
flow dataset used

00:16:52.736 --> 00:16:53.524
in our experiment.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:57.590
All three data sources concern network traffic,
but they define

00:16:57.602 --> 00:16:58.828
different learning problems.

00:16:59.241 --> 00:17:04.031
Your C I C I D S extract has fifteen packet
label names.

00:17:04.444 --> 00:17:07.045
Your U N S W extract has ten.

00:17:07.445 --> 00:17:12.486
The authors&#x27; compatible C I C IoT twenty twenty
two flow release

00:17:12.686 --> 00:17:14.137
has six class meanings.

00:17:14.524 --> 00:17:18.652
A classifier&#x27;s output layer is tied to its
training labels.

00:17:19.065 --> 00:17:22.917
Six output numbers cannot simply be relabeled
with a new ten

00:17:22.929 --> 00:17:24.243
category vocabulary.

00:17:24.655 --> 00:17:27.520
Changing the dataset can require a new class
mapping,

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:31.547
classification head, training protocol, and
evaluation.

00:17:31.947 --> 00:17:35.649
The repository checks that a saved classifier
stays attached to

00:17:35.649 --> 00:17:37.150
the correct class meanings.

00:17:37.633 --> 00:17:41.584
To exercise the original Net Mamba Plus path, we
acquired the

00:17:41.647 --> 00:17:45.935
authors&#x27; native flow release rather than
silently fabricate flow

00:17:45.935 --> 00:17:47.786
context for the CSV rows.

00:17:48.186 --> 00:17:51.336
The training split has eight thousand three
hundred twenty three

00:17:51.336 --> 00:17:55.737
flows, validation has one thousand forty, and
the test split has

00:17:55.737 --> 00:17:57.063
one thousand forty one.

00:17:57.475 --> 00:18:01.701
Those three files total ten thousand four
hundred four flows.

00:18:02.114 --> 00:18:04.164
The provided split boundaries were retained.

00:18:04.577 --> 00:18:08.378
This choice makes the original loader and
classifier executable

00:18:08.565 --> 00:18:09.878
with meaningful inputs.

00:18:10.278 --> 00:18:14.004
It also means the actual training result is not
a result on

00:18:14.166 --> 00:18:15.904
either of your uploaded CSVs.

00:18:16.304 --> 00:18:19.193
That distinction belongs in the customer
presentation.

00:18:19.654 --> 00:18:23.680
Compatibility is not a guarantee of independent
evaluation.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:27.856
The validation report found five identical
stored inputs shared

00:18:27.856 --> 00:18:31.781
between training and validation, and six shared
between training

00:18:31.894 --> 00:18:32.556
and test.

00:18:33.007 --> 00:18:37.720
File names and hashes identify records, but they
cannot establish

00:18:37.732 --> 00:18:39.870
that all physical captures are independent.

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:43.846
The released pretrained checkpoint&#x27;s earlier
exposure is also

00:18:43.921 --> 00:18:44.421
unknown.

00:18:44.821 --> 00:18:48.534
We preserved the authors&#x27; splits and disclosed
these issues.

00:18:48.922 --> 00:18:53.373
A stronger future generalization study would
define a new capture

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:56.261
independent holdout and document its provenance.

00:18:56.661 --> 00:18:59.774
Repeating inference on the existing test file
checks

00:18:59.824 --> 00:19:03.800
reproducibility of that result; it does not
create a new holdout.

00:19:04.267 --> 00:19:07.480
This is the connection between your original
three files and the

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:08.280
repository.

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:11.131
The PDF provides the research method.

00:19:11.518 --> 00:19:15.519
The CSVs provide packet records that can be
profiled, but do not

00:19:15.519 --> 00:19:18.070
establish the method&#x27;s required flow context.

00:19:18.482 --> 00:19:22.421
The repository therefore preserves that finding
and runs a

00:19:22.433 --> 00:19:25.021
compatible native flow experiment instead.

00:19:25.434 --> 00:19:29.035
If you later obtain raw captures with
trustworthy grouping and

00:19:29.035 --> 00:19:32.948
extraction rules, you can investigate a matching
data preparation

00:19:32.948 --> 00:19:33.448
path.

00:19:33.848 --> 00:19:37.624
Alternatively, you can explicitly design a
packet classifier

00:19:37.624 --> 00:19:38.312
adaptation.

00:19:38.725 --> 00:19:42.713
Take ten seconds to explain which data was
actually used for the

00:19:42.725 --> 00:19:44.638
published training runs, and why.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:55.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Which data was
actually used for training, and why were the
uploaded CSVs not used?

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:58.846
The actual experiment uses the authors&#x27; native
flow files.

00:19:59.259 --> 00:20:02.253
Each file contains a JSON array of flow objects.

00:20:02.666 --> 00:20:06.161
JSON is structured text with named fields and
lists.

00:20:06.575 --> 00:20:09.406
The data field holds a list of packet byte
strings.

00:20:09.794 --> 00:20:13.590
Sizes and intervals are space separated numeric
strings.

00:20:13.991 --> 00:20:16.847
Labeled examples also carry class information.

00:20:17.248 --> 00:20:20.216
The small example on screen is synthetic and
deliberately

00:20:20.229 --> 00:20:22.158
shortened to explain the schema.

00:20:22.559 --> 00:20:24.325
It is not a recovered packet capture.

00:20:24.739 --> 00:20:27.833
The real loader converts these fields into fixed
numerical

00:20:27.845 --> 00:20:32.017
arrays, so variable length flows can enter the
same neural model.

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:36.356
The byte branch uses the first five packets and
up to three

00:20:36.368 --> 00:20:38.246
hundred twenty stored bytes from each.

00:20:38.697 --> 00:20:41.688
Five times three hundred twenty gives sixteen
hundred byte

00:20:41.701 --> 00:20:42.414
positions.

00:20:42.815 --> 00:20:46.758
Short inputs receive padding and long inputs are
truncated.

00:20:47.159 --> 00:20:50.777
The paper describes an eighty byte header
allocation and a two

00:20:50.777 --> 00:20:53.430
hundred forty byte payload allocation per
packet.

00:20:53.831 --> 00:20:56.785
The released stored bytes can be loaded, but
their complete

00:20:56.785 --> 00:21:00.403
extraction history has not been independently
verified.

00:21:00.803 --> 00:21:04.471
We therefore say stored bytes when describing
what our executed

00:21:04.471 --> 00:21:06.036
loader actually consumed.

00:21:06.449 --> 00:21:09.241
This preserves the difference between a paper&#x27;s
intended

00:21:09.241 --> 00:21:12.683
representation and a release&#x27;s established
provenance.

00:21:13.167 --> 00:21:16.448
The loader scales a byte value by dividing by
two hundred fifty

00:21:16.448 --> 00:21:16.962
five.

00:21:17.375 --> 00:21:20.418
It then subtracts one half and divides by one
half.

00:21:20.819 --> 00:21:24.577
Zero becomes negative one, and two hundred fifty
five becomes

00:21:24.577 --> 00:21:25.403
positive one.

00:21:25.804 --> 00:21:28.597
This puts byte inputs on a common numerical
scale.

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:32.367
A tensor is simply a multidimensional array of
numbers.

00:21:32.818 --> 00:21:37.101
For a batch of flows, the byte tensor has
dimensions batch, one,

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:39.168
one, and sixteen hundred.

00:21:39.568 --> 00:21:43.113
The extra dimensions match the original model&#x27;s
interface.

00:21:43.476 --> 00:21:47.258
A matching shape is necessary for execution, but
it still does

00:21:47.271 --> 00:21:50.339
not prove that the underlying bytes mean the
right thing.

00:21:50.813 --> 00:21:54.394
The size branch uses the first twenty packet
sizes, clipped

00:21:54.394 --> 00:21:56.585
between zero and fifteen hundred.

00:21:56.986 --> 00:21:59.615
Its padding value is clipped to the same upper
limit.

00:22:00.029 --> 00:22:03.735
The timing branch uses the first twenty gaps and
maps an observed

00:22:03.735 --> 00:22:07.842
gap x to one plus x divided by two plus x.

00:22:08.343 --> 00:22:12.049
Zero maps to one half; one maps to two thirds.

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:16.019
Those are numerical examples, not an assertion
about seconds.

00:22:16.432 --> 00:22:19.224
Missing intervals use padding that maps to one.

00:22:19.612 --> 00:22:23.319
The wrapper requires actual observed gaps to be
finite and

00:22:23.331 --> 00:22:24.020
nonnegative.

00:22:24.408 --> 00:22:28.390
Training and inference must use the same rules
or they present

00:22:28.402 --> 00:22:29.842
different problems to the model.

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:33.731
A token here is one input unit for the sequence
model.

00:22:34.144 --> 00:22:35.822
It is not a word from a language model.

00:22:36.223 --> 00:22:39.478
The sixteen hundred byte positions are grouped
into four byte

00:22:39.478 --> 00:22:42.633
strides, producing four hundred byte tokens.

00:22:43.047 --> 00:22:47.467
Twenty size tokens and twenty interval tokens
add forty more.

00:22:47.917 --> 00:22:51.611
Three learned summary tokens bring the total to
four hundred

00:22:51.611 --> 00:22:52.262
forty three.

00:22:52.675 --> 00:22:56.644
Each token is embedded into a vector with two
hundred fifty six

00:22:56.644 --> 00:22:57.296
coordinates.

00:22:57.684 --> 00:23:00.000
An embedding is a numerical representation.

00:23:00.413 --> 00:23:04.007
The model learns how useful patterns in those
coordinates relate

00:23:04.107 --> 00:23:05.534
to the dataset categories.

00:23:05.988 --> 00:23:08.893
The sequence passes through four Mamba blocks.

00:23:09.294 --> 00:23:13.139
The active configuration uses the unidirectional
branch and its

00:23:13.139 --> 00:23:14.905
fast execution path.

00:23:15.305 --> 00:23:18.875
After processing, the three summary vectors are
added together

00:23:19.275 --> 00:23:21.191
and passed to the classification head.

00:23:21.592 --> 00:23:26.188
The result is six logits, or raw category
scores, for each flow.

00:23:26.602 --> 00:23:31.223
The six class meanings are Flood, R T S P brute
force, Audio,

00:23:31.511 --> 00:23:33.778
Other, Cameras, and Home Automation.

00:23:34.216 --> 00:23:37.961
Four describe device or activity groups, so
these are not six

00:23:37.961 --> 00:23:38.850
attack types.

00:23:39.263 --> 00:23:42.770
The complete classifier has one million eight
hundred seventy

00:23:42.782 --> 00:23:44.486
thousand eighty parameters.

00:23:44.899 --> 00:23:47.955
Parameters are the learned numbers updated
during training.

00:23:48.355 --> 00:23:50.597
They are different from the input tensor values.

00:23:50.998 --> 00:23:54.868
The runtime checks exercise this complete model
rather than

00:23:54.868 --> 00:23:56.984
replacing it with a smaller lookalike.

00:23:57.434 --> 00:24:00.452
To explain the input contract, cover three
levels.

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:03.032
The fields must describe the correct flow.

00:24:03.433 --> 00:24:07.090
Their dimensions and transformations must match
the original

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:07.503
loader.

00:24:07.954 --> 00:24:12.300
Finally, the label vocabulary must match the
saved classifier.

00:24:12.701 --> 00:24:16.646
A file can pass a basic JSON parser and still
fail one of these

00:24:16.646 --> 00:24:17.410
requirements.

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:22.557
Our wrappers reject incompatible or invalid
inputs before model

00:24:22.557 --> 00:24:25.112
execution where those conditions can be checked.

00:24:25.525 --> 00:24:29.796
Take ten seconds to describe the three feature
views and the six

00:24:29.796 --> 00:24:33.591
number output, without using the word artificial
intelligence as

00:24:33.591 --> 00:24:35.645
a substitute for the actual process.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:46.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Describe the three
feature views and the six-number output.

00:24:46.113 --> 00:24:50.652
Bytes, sizes, and intervals enter the model; six
raw scores come

00:24:50.652 --> 00:24:50.965
out.

00:24:51.366 --> 00:24:54.200
Now separate learning from using what was
learned.

00:24:54.614 --> 00:24:58.726
Pretraining teaches a representation from a
reconstruction task.

00:24:59.177 --> 00:25:03.528
Fine tuning adapts that representation to known
categories using

00:25:03.528 --> 00:25:04.506
labeled examples.

00:25:04.907 --> 00:25:08.343
Inference uses a fixed classifier to produce
scores without

00:25:08.443 --> 00:25:09.772
updating its parameters.

00:25:10.186 --> 00:25:13.872
A checkpoint saves model state so a later
process can reload it.

00:25:14.273 --> 00:25:17.746
Some training checkpoints also include optimizer
state and

00:25:17.746 --> 00:25:18.411
settings.

00:25:18.824 --> 00:25:22.398
The word checkpoint therefore describes a file&#x27;s
role, not a

00:25:22.398 --> 00:25:25.231
guarantee that it contains a complete deployment
package.

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:29.498
During masked pretraining, parts of the input
are hidden or

00:25:29.498 --> 00:25:32.030
zeroed according to the original procedure.

00:25:32.419 --> 00:25:36.279
A reconstruction decoder tries to recover the
missing content and

00:25:36.279 --> 00:25:37.295
feature values.

00:25:37.708 --> 00:25:39.701
The loss measures reconstruction error.

00:25:40.102 --> 00:25:43.562
The optimizer adjusts parameters to reduce that
error over

00:25:43.562 --> 00:25:44.239
examples.

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:48.350
Labels such as Flood or Cameras are not the
reconstruction

00:25:48.350 --> 00:25:48.801
target.

00:25:49.203 --> 00:25:52.599
Learning a useful representation this way may
help a later

00:25:52.612 --> 00:25:56.573
classification task, but a falling
reconstruction loss does not

00:25:56.585 --> 00:25:59.092
itself prove attack detection accuracy.

00:25:59.506 --> 00:26:03.492
The full paper uses a much larger pretraining
protocol than the

00:26:03.492 --> 00:26:05.385
short functional check we executed.

00:26:05.854 --> 00:26:09.492
Our limited check completed two epochs and one
hundred thirty two

00:26:09.605 --> 00:26:12.226
optimizer updates on downstream training data.

00:26:12.628 --> 00:26:16.742
Reconstruction loss fell from about nine point
five two to six

00:26:16.742 --> 00:26:17.745
point one three.

00:26:18.146 --> 00:26:21.734
The requested short step budget was rounded by
the original loop

00:26:21.934 --> 00:26:23.088
to complete epochs.

00:26:23.477 --> 00:26:26.814
This demonstrates that the masked training path
executes and

00:26:26.814 --> 00:26:27.967
learns on that check.

00:26:28.369 --> 00:26:31.743
It does not recreate the paper&#x27;s one hundred
fifty thousand step

00:26:31.755 --> 00:26:32.470
pretraining.

00:26:32.872 --> 00:26:35.970
The three main fine tuning runs started from the
authors&#x27;

00:26:35.982 --> 00:26:39.407
released pretrained checkpoint, not the output
of our short

00:26:39.407 --> 00:26:39.833
check.

00:26:40.234 --> 00:26:43.684
Keep those two branches separate when drawing
the workflow.

00:26:44.134 --> 00:26:48.047
In fine tuning, a batch contains multiple
labeled flows.

00:26:48.473 --> 00:26:50.806
The forward pass computes category scores.

00:26:51.207 --> 00:26:54.944
A classification loss compares those scores with
the supplied

00:26:54.944 --> 00:26:57.127
labels, with the configured smoothing.

00:26:57.528 --> 00:27:00.901
The backward pass computes gradients, which
describe how

00:27:00.914 --> 00:27:03.134
parameter changes affect the loss.

00:27:03.548 --> 00:27:06.206
The optimizer then updates those parameters.

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:10.508
Repeating this process across the training
loader forms an epoch.

00:27:10.897 --> 00:27:13.631
A learning rate controls the scale of updates.

00:27:14.032 --> 00:27:18.071
This is why training needs much more computation
than simply

00:27:18.083 --> 00:27:19.513
opening the recorded demo.

00:27:19.914 --> 00:27:24.304
The demo reads saved outputs; a training command
repeatedly

00:27:24.316 --> 00:27:25.282
changes the model.

00:27:25.767 --> 00:27:28.286
The training split supplies parameter updates.

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:31.069
Validation results select a checkpoint.

00:27:31.495 --> 00:27:34.904
The test split measures the selected
classifier&#x27;s performance

00:27:34.917 --> 00:27:35.393
afterward.

00:27:35.794 --> 00:27:38.915
Choosing a checkpoint by its test score would
use the test set as

00:27:38.915 --> 00:27:39.755
a tuning signal.

00:27:40.168 --> 00:27:44.029
The native fine tuner selects the best
validation accuracy and

00:27:44.029 --> 00:27:46.159
then performs its final test pass.

00:27:46.573 --> 00:27:51.173
Separate evaluation strictly reloads that
selected classifier.

00:27:51.586 --> 00:27:55.685
This separation is necessary, although the
previously disclosed

00:27:55.798 --> 00:28:00.310
input overlaps and unknown capture history still
limit the

00:28:00.322 --> 00:28:00.911
evidence.

00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:05.988
Following the split procedure cannot undo
uncertainty in how the

00:28:06.063 --> 00:28:07.955
underlying data was collected.

00:28:08.421 --> 00:28:12.397
Each of the three declared seeds completed one
hundred twenty

00:28:12.397 --> 00:28:15.846
epochs and seven thousand nine hundred twenty
updates.

00:28:16.247 --> 00:28:20.261
A seed sets the starting values for randomized
operations; the

00:28:20.261 --> 00:28:22.719
seeds are not three independent datasets.

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:27.008
The source based recipe uses batch size one
hundred twenty eight

00:28:27.284 --> 00:28:30.608
and a base learning rate that resolves to an
effective rate of

00:28:30.608 --> 00:28:32.501
zero point zero zero one.

00:28:32.903 --> 00:28:36.414
The paper describes different batch and runtime
conditions.

00:28:36.816 --> 00:28:40.804
Our training ran without mixed precision, while
native evaluation

00:28:41.118 --> 00:28:43.425
used its recorded mixed precision path.

00:28:43.827 --> 00:28:47.363
These details help another engineer reproduce
our execution

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:51.151
rather than accidentally claim an exact paper
configuration.

00:28:51.634 --> 00:28:56.061
Strict reloading rejects missing or unexpected
model tensors

00:28:56.362 --> 00:28:58.168
rather than quietly ignoring them.

00:28:58.569 --> 00:29:01.981
The wrapper also checks the class meaning
associated with each

00:29:01.981 --> 00:29:02.959
output index.

00:29:03.373 --> 00:29:06.446
This matters because a numerically valid
classifier could

00:29:06.458 --> 00:29:09.155
otherwise display the wrong category names.

00:29:09.556 --> 00:29:13.983
Inference can return logits, display scores, and
a selected class

00:29:13.996 --> 00:29:15.451
for unlabeled flows.

00:29:15.865 --> 00:29:19.602
It cannot calculate accuracy without ground
truth.

00:29:20.004 --> 00:29:23.879
Take ten seconds: explain the difference between
demonstrating a

00:29:23.892 --> 00:29:27.817
short pretraining update, completing fine
tuning, and using a

00:29:27.817 --> 00:29:29.498
saved classifier to predict.

00:29:29.887 --> 00:29:32.633
Name the separate output produced by each
activity.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:43.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] What does each of
pretraining, fine-tuning and inference produce?

00:29:43.113 --> 00:29:46.963
Pretraining produces representation weights,
fine tuning produces

00:29:46.975 --> 00:29:51.000
a task specific classifier, and inference
produces predictions.

00:29:51.402 --> 00:29:54.574
Now follow the code that organizes those
activities.

00:29:54.988 --> 00:29:59.063
This repository is an execution and evidence
layer around the

00:29:59.076 --> 00:30:00.142
authors&#x27; source.

00:30:00.556 --> 00:30:03.114
It does not claim authorship of Net Mamba Plus.

00:30:03.515 --> 00:30:06.838
The source is fetched at a pinned commit, with a
recorded

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:10.650
inventory of one hundred three source files and
additional core

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:11.252
hashes.

00:30:11.653 --> 00:30:14.675
Keeping the original loader avoids writing a
second

00:30:14.675 --> 00:30:17.785
interpretation of byte padding and
normalization.

00:30:18.199 --> 00:30:22.487
The surrounding code supplies validation,
reproducible commands,

00:30:22.726 --> 00:30:25.183
manifests, and independent output checks.

00:30:25.667 --> 00:30:28.313
The main entry point is repro dot p y.

00:30:28.714 --> 00:30:32.263
Its commands acquire or validate the pinned
source and data,

00:30:32.752 --> 00:30:36.037
resolve the original command line settings, and
launch training

00:30:36.049 --> 00:30:37.015
subprocesses.

00:30:37.416 --> 00:30:41.266
A subprocess is a separately running program
invoked by the

00:30:41.266 --> 00:30:41.968
coordinator.

00:30:42.369 --> 00:30:46.432
The wrapper creates a fresh output directory and
a manifest

00:30:46.444 --> 00:30:50.683
recording identities, configuration,
environment, and status.

00:30:51.084 --> 00:30:53.730
A failed job should leave an informative record.

00:30:54.143 --> 00:30:57.943
It should not overwrite a prior successful
result and make the

00:30:57.955 --> 00:31:00.350
two runs impossible to distinguish.

00:31:00.752 --> 00:31:04.463
Shared preparation helpers are reused by the
other entry points.

00:31:04.934 --> 00:31:10.112
Evaluate dot p y loads a saved classifier
strictly and evaluates

00:31:10.125 --> 00:31:14.099
labeled test flows through the original loader
and evaluation

00:31:14.099 --> 00:31:14.413
engine.

00:31:14.814 --> 00:31:16.168
It does not train the model again.

00:31:16.569 --> 00:31:20.782
It also does not choose a checkpoint by
maximizing test accuracy.

00:31:21.196 --> 00:31:25.121
Its result is a set of metrics attached to the
evaluated model

00:31:25.133 --> 00:31:25.685
and data.

00:31:26.086 --> 00:31:29.233
If someone sends you a number without a
checkpoint identity or

00:31:29.484 --> 00:31:32.594
class mapping, you cannot establish that it
describes the

00:31:32.606 --> 00:31:33.910
intended classifier.

00:31:34.324 --> 00:31:38.399
This wrapper records those bindings so the
reported accuracy can

00:31:38.399 --> 00:31:40.443
be traced to a particular execution.

00:31:40.913 --> 00:31:44.864
Predict dot p y accepts native flows whose true
categories are

00:31:44.864 --> 00:31:45.341
unknown.

00:31:45.730 --> 00:31:49.217
It uses the original loader, discards targets
before the model

00:31:49.217 --> 00:31:53.219
runs, and returns a class name, logits, and raw
scores.

00:31:53.633 --> 00:31:57.271
The new calibration module can also divide those
logits by one

00:31:57.271 --> 00:32:00.846
positive number called a temperature, then
compute a second set

00:32:00.846 --> 00:32:01.473
of scores.

00:32:01.875 --> 00:32:06.265
The largest logit stays largest, so the
predicted class does not

00:32:06.265 --> 00:32:07.959
improve or change.

00:32:08.373 --> 00:32:12.663
Tools slash calibrate dot p y fits that number
from validation

00:32:12.675 --> 00:32:13.215
labels.

00:32:13.641 --> 00:32:17.643
The calibration file records the checkpoint,
configuration, class

00:32:17.656 --> 00:32:19.939
meanings, and runtime that it belongs to.

00:32:20.388 --> 00:32:24.313
Replay dot p y records labeled model predictions
and

00:32:24.363 --> 00:32:27.334
independently recomputes metrics from the saved
outputs.

00:32:27.748 --> 00:32:31.134
It also builds the self contained browser
demonstration.

00:32:31.548 --> 00:32:35.184
The browser page displays the data already
written into it.

00:32:35.573 --> 00:32:38.946
It does not need CUDA or a model checkpoint to
replay those

00:32:38.946 --> 00:32:39.548
results.

00:32:39.961 --> 00:32:43.585
This split is intentional: the numerical
experiment can be

00:32:43.585 --> 00:32:47.046
reviewed once and then explained on another
computer without

00:32:47.059 --> 00:32:47.949
rerunning training.

00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:51.899
The tradeoff is that the page is a recorded
demonstration.

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:55.535
New customer flows require actual inference
through a checked

00:32:55.548 --> 00:32:58.331
runtime, not just a browser refresh.

00:32:58.800 --> 00:33:02.287
The configuration files are recipes and identity
checks.

00:33:02.701 --> 00:33:07.228
They contain settings, source pins, expected
asset hashes, and

00:33:07.241 --> 00:33:08.181
class metadata.

00:33:08.582 --> 00:33:12.646
The assets directory holds separately acquired
data and weights

00:33:12.658 --> 00:33:13.260
on your machine.

00:33:13.662 --> 00:33:17.888
The downloaded authors&#x27; source lives in the
local upstream slash

00:33:17.901 --> 00:33:19.957
Net Mamba Plus directory.

00:33:20.359 --> 00:33:24.071
Those external assets are not mirrored as public
repository

00:33:24.071 --> 00:33:24.811
contents.

00:33:25.212 --> 00:33:28.874
This distinction explains why cloning the
repository is enough

00:33:28.887 --> 00:33:32.122
for document checks but not enough for neural
training.

00:33:32.536 --> 00:33:36.725
The acquisition tools must obtain the additional
files, verify

00:33:36.738 --> 00:33:39.998
their identities, and preserve their unresolved
inherited

00:33:40.011 --> 00:33:42.130
redistribution and commercial terms.

00:33:42.600 --> 00:33:45.322
When auditing a run, start with its manifest.

00:33:45.724 --> 00:33:48.947
Then inspect the log and the saved metrics or
predictions.

00:33:49.361 --> 00:33:52.999
A configuration file proves what was requested,
not that the run

00:33:52.999 --> 00:33:53.588
completed.

00:33:53.965 --> 00:33:58.091
A model file proves saved state exists, not
which data produced

00:33:58.104 --> 00:33:58.255
it.

00:33:58.668 --> 00:34:02.068
A chart is a presentation of measurements, not
their origin.

00:34:02.482 --> 00:34:03.761
The records work together.

00:34:04.163 --> 00:34:07.549
Take ten seconds to decide which entry point you
would use for an

00:34:07.612 --> 00:34:10.660
unlabeled native flow, and which one you would
use to score a

00:34:10.673 --> 00:34:11.626
labeled test set.

00:34:12.027 --> 00:34:15.627
Then identify the file that records the run&#x27;s
provenance.

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:26.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Which scripts
handle unlabeled prediction and labeled
evaluation? Where is run provenance recorded?

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:30.595
Use predict for unlabeled flows, evaluate for
labeled scoring,

00:34:30.871 --> 00:34:32.661
and the manifest for run provenance.

00:34:33.062 --> 00:34:35.090
Now examine the recorded results.

00:34:35.491 --> 00:34:40.462
Seed zero correctly classified nine hundred
fifty of one thousand

00:34:40.462 --> 00:34:42.027
forty one test flows.

00:34:42.440 --> 00:34:44.982
Seed one got eight hundred seventy five correct.

00:34:45.383 --> 00:34:47.286
Seed two got eight hundred eighty one.

00:34:47.699 --> 00:34:51.894
Those correspond to ninety one point two six,
eighty four point

00:34:51.894 --> 00:34:56.313
zero five, and eighty four point six three
percent accuracy.

00:34:56.702 --> 00:34:58.142
All three results are retained.

00:34:58.542 --> 00:35:01.510
Selecting only the strongest seed would give a
misleading

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:05.304
impression of this training procedure&#x27;s observed
variability.

00:35:05.767 --> 00:35:09.572
The average accuracy is eighty six point six
five percent.

00:35:09.973 --> 00:35:13.177
The sample standard deviation is four percentage
points.

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:17.796
The mean summarizes these three runs; the
standard deviation

00:35:18.084 --> 00:35:19.398
summarizes their spread.

00:35:19.799 --> 00:35:22.652
It is not a confidence interval and does not
prove how

00:35:22.652 --> 00:35:24.981
performance will vary on a customer&#x27;s network.

00:35:25.381 --> 00:35:28.022
All three seeds used the same test split.

00:35:28.448 --> 00:35:32.341
One result has therefore been repeated under
different randomized

00:35:32.353 --> 00:35:36.459
training conditions, rather than evaluated on
three independently

00:35:36.471 --> 00:35:37.573
collected networks.

00:35:37.973 --> 00:35:40.840
Those are useful measurements, but they answer a
narrower

00:35:40.840 --> 00:35:43.193
question than real world generalization.

00:35:43.654 --> 00:35:47.773
Accuracy counts correct predictions across the
full test set.

00:35:48.174 --> 00:35:51.654
Precision asks how many predictions of a
category were right.

00:35:52.042 --> 00:35:56.186
Recall asks how many actual examples of that
category were found.

00:35:56.587 --> 00:35:59.115
F one combines precision and recall.

00:35:59.516 --> 00:36:03.823
Macro F one treats categories equally, while
weighted F one

00:36:03.823 --> 00:36:07.628
accounts for how many true examples each
category has.

00:36:08.029 --> 00:36:12.085
These metrics can disagree when class sizes
differ or errors

00:36:12.085 --> 00:36:13.888
concentrate in one category.

00:36:14.301 --> 00:36:18.069
The repository preserves independent metrics and
a confusion

00:36:18.069 --> 00:36:21.762
matrix so you can inspect the pattern, rather
than trust a single

00:36:21.762 --> 00:36:22.501
percentage.

00:36:22.954 --> 00:36:27.713
In the matrix, each row is a known category and
each column is

00:36:27.725 --> 00:36:28.690
the model&#x27;s prediction.

00:36:29.103 --> 00:36:30.944
Counts on the diagonal are correct.

00:36:31.357 --> 00:36:35.464
Off diagonal counts show which categories were
confused.

00:36:35.853 --> 00:36:39.760
Our test set has two hundred examples for each
category except

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:41.388
Other, which has forty one.

00:36:41.788 --> 00:36:44.906
That support difference matters when comparing
percentages.

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:48.826
The matrix is computed from saved predictions,
so another

00:36:48.839 --> 00:36:50.116
reviewer can recompute it.

00:36:50.517 --> 00:36:53.797
A large diagonal is encouraging, but the
mistakes remain

00:36:53.810 --> 00:36:58.256
important: an operational system must understand
the cost of both

00:36:58.256 --> 00:37:01.649
missed attacks and incorrect alerts on benign
activity.

00:37:02.146 --> 00:37:05.088
The testing was rerun on the actual G B ten.

00:37:05.488 --> 00:37:09.494
Seven CUDA operator comparisons passed against
reference

00:37:09.494 --> 00:37:10.358
calculations.

00:37:10.759 --> 00:37:14.577
The complete original model produced finite
gradients, changed

00:37:14.577 --> 00:37:19.158
its weights in an optimizer step, saved, and
reloaded strictly.

00:37:19.572 --> 00:37:23.728
This was a functionality test using six
synthetic fixtures, not

00:37:23.728 --> 00:37:25.480
another full training experiment.

00:37:25.918 --> 00:37:30.625
Three fresh seed zero inference runs also
retained every one of

00:37:30.625 --> 00:37:33.692
the one thousand forty one recorded class
predictions.

00:37:34.105 --> 00:37:37.272
Some half precision logits differed slightly
from the older

00:37:37.285 --> 00:37:41.141
recording, by at most zero point zero zero three
nine zero six

00:37:41.153 --> 00:37:41.792
two five.

00:37:42.205 --> 00:37:45.234
We preserve that difference rather than promise
bitwise

00:37:45.322 --> 00:37:46.311
reproducibility.

00:37:46.779 --> 00:37:49.548
The paper comparison is higher than our measured
result.

00:37:49.961 --> 00:37:53.656
Possible contributing factors include runtime
differences,

00:37:53.919 --> 00:37:58.040
training settings, extraction details, and
checkpoint history,

00:37:58.491 --> 00:38:00.833
but we have not isolated their effects.

00:38:01.234 --> 00:38:05.029
Naming plausible causes is not the same as
testing them.

00:38:05.417 --> 00:38:09.238
A defensible follow up would change one relevant
factor under a

00:38:09.238 --> 00:38:12.169
controlled protocol and retain every outcome.

00:38:12.582 --> 00:38:16.014
It would also address data independence and
record exact

00:38:16.014 --> 00:38:16.966
preprocessing.

00:38:17.392 --> 00:38:21.387
For now the accurate statement is that the model
executes and

00:38:21.387 --> 00:38:24.794
trains, while the paper&#x27;s headline accuracy has
not been

00:38:24.794 --> 00:38:26.949
reproduced under our measured conditions.

00:38:27.434 --> 00:38:31.141
All three confidence measures improved for every
checkpoint.

00:38:31.529 --> 00:38:35.311
Mean negative log likelihood fell from zero
point four four one

00:38:35.323 --> 00:38:38.793
six to zero point four one seven two.

00:38:39.181 --> 00:38:44.090
Mean Brier score fell from zero point two zero
three five to zero

00:38:44.090 --> 00:38:46.144
point two zero one two.

00:38:46.532 --> 00:38:50.252
Expected calibration error fell from seven point
zero three

00:38:50.264 --> 00:38:52.944
percent to three point eight two percent.

00:38:53.345 --> 00:38:55.637
Lower is better for these measures.

00:38:56.013 --> 00:38:58.317
Accuracy stayed unchanged.

00:38:58.730 --> 00:39:02.876
This retrospective comparison reuses validation
and already seen

00:39:02.876 --> 00:39:03.577
test data.

00:39:03.978 --> 00:39:09.238
Take ten seconds to explain our mean accuracy
and spread without

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:10.615
claiming customer validation.

00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:21.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Explain the mean
and spread without claiming customer-network
validation.

00:39:21.100 --> 00:39:24.048
Now use the browser replay to make the result
concrete.

00:39:24.462 --> 00:39:27.047
The page can be opened on a presentation machine
without

00:39:27.059 --> 00:39:30.421
installing CUDA, Python, or a checkpoint.

00:39:30.823 --> 00:39:34.699
It displays seed zero&#x27;s saved predictions and
their known labels.

00:39:35.113 --> 00:39:38.952
Its controls let you inspect the recorded
sequence and errors.

00:39:39.379 --> 00:39:41.624
The screenshot comes from the actual working
page.

00:39:42.038 --> 00:39:45.450
This is a good customer demonstration because
the evidence is

00:39:45.450 --> 00:39:48.825
available offline and can be inspected
consistently.

00:39:49.226 --> 00:39:53.768
Say recorded replay before presenting it, so a
moving table is

00:39:53.768 --> 00:39:56.239
not mistaken for a live packet feed.

00:39:56.700 --> 00:39:59.810
Seed zero was declared as the demonstration
model.

00:40:00.223 --> 00:40:02.743
The other two results remain published beside
it.

00:40:03.119 --> 00:40:07.796
A demo often needs one fixed model and a
manageable story, but

00:40:07.808 --> 00:40:10.090
that choice must not hide variability.

00:40:10.504 --> 00:40:14.629
Also, the number of predictions assigned to
attack categories is

00:40:14.641 --> 00:40:17.199
not the number of operational security alerts.

00:40:17.575 --> 00:40:21.223
A real alert policy might combine repeated
observations,

00:40:21.587 --> 00:40:25.085
thresholds, exceptions, and analyst context.

00:40:25.486 --> 00:40:28.458
Those policy components have not been
implemented here.

00:40:28.859 --> 00:40:33.021
The recorded page lets you explain
classification behavior; it

00:40:33.021 --> 00:40:35.993
does not establish an incident response
workflow.

00:40:36.454 --> 00:40:40.078
Look at the saved prediction with JSON index six
hundred thirty

00:40:40.090 --> 00:40:40.579
five.

00:40:40.981 --> 00:40:44.855
The browser numbers rows from one, so it
displays that record as

00:40:44.867 --> 00:40:46.522
zero six three six.

00:40:46.936 --> 00:40:50.196
The highest score belongs to R T S P brute
force.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:54.308
Its normalized display score is eighty four
point four three

00:40:54.308 --> 00:40:54.860
percent.

00:40:55.274 --> 00:40:58.947
Before looking at the true label, notice how
confident that

00:40:58.947 --> 00:41:00.251
display can appear.

00:41:00.653 --> 00:41:04.251
Softmax converts six logits into scores that add
to one.

00:41:04.677 --> 00:41:08.175
That arithmetic does not establish that the top
score is a

00:41:08.175 --> 00:41:10.382
calibrated probability of an attack.

00:41:10.880 --> 00:41:12.335
The known label is Other.

00:41:12.749 --> 00:41:14.229
This prediction is wrong.

00:41:14.643 --> 00:41:18.607
That single record gives you a useful way to
explain why a high

00:41:18.607 --> 00:41:22.420
display score is not sufficient evidence to
block traffic.

00:41:22.834 --> 00:41:26.309
The model must assign one of its known
categories, even when a

00:41:26.309 --> 00:41:29.985
flow is ambiguous or unlike its training
examples.

00:41:30.399 --> 00:41:33.698
The project has no validated unknown attack
detector.

00:41:34.112 --> 00:41:38.277
Its new calibration results do not establish
reliability on

00:41:38.289 --> 00:41:39.280
customer traffic.

00:41:39.694 --> 00:41:44.373
This real mistake exposes the gap between
classifier output and

00:41:44.386 --> 00:41:46.154
an operational security decision.

00:41:46.634 --> 00:41:50.335
To use the new feature, first run the
calibration command on

00:41:50.335 --> 00:41:52.593
validation flows with the frozen checkpoint.

00:41:53.007 --> 00:41:56.432
Then supply that calibration file to predict dot
p y.

00:41:56.821 --> 00:42:00.634
A threshold of zero point nine accepts a result
only when its

00:42:00.647 --> 00:42:04.574
calibrated top class score reaches that value;
lower scores are

00:42:04.586 --> 00:42:05.364
marked defer.

00:42:05.765 --> 00:42:08.864
The original prediction remains in both cases.

00:42:09.266 --> 00:42:13.606
Missing, malformed, or incompatible calibration
files cause an

00:42:13.619 --> 00:42:14.723
explicit failure.

00:42:15.124 --> 00:42:18.223
These are new model executions outside the
browser.

00:42:18.624 --> 00:42:22.313
This interface is a useful component for a
future service, but

00:42:22.313 --> 00:42:26.177
capture, flow creation, and customer data
validation still need

00:42:26.177 --> 00:42:27.068
to be built around it.

00:42:27.546 --> 00:42:29.980
Here is the tradeoff behind that threshold.

00:42:30.419 --> 00:42:34.772
For seed zero, the raw scores accepted six
hundred twenty flows,

00:42:34.997 --> 00:42:35.863
with nine wrong.

00:42:36.277 --> 00:42:40.692
Calibrated scores accepted eight hundred ninety
three flows, with

00:42:40.692 --> 00:42:41.734
thirty seven wrong.

00:42:42.135 --> 00:42:45.773
So the calibrated policy deferred fewer
predictions, while

00:42:45.785 --> 00:42:47.215
accepting more errors.

00:42:47.617 --> 00:42:49.875
Acceptance is not a safety guarantee.

00:42:50.264 --> 00:42:54.855
Coverage means accepted divided by all rows;
accepted error means

00:42:54.855 --> 00:42:57.213
wrong accepted divided by accepted.

00:42:57.614 --> 00:43:01.892
When nothing is accepted, that error is
undefined, not zero.

00:43:02.293 --> 00:43:06.144
This is a fixed demonstration threshold on
already seen research

00:43:06.144 --> 00:43:06.583
data.

00:43:06.972 --> 00:43:11.237
Capture, independently labeled customer traffic,
and operational

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:13.759
response rules still need validation.

00:43:14.234 --> 00:43:17.844
To hand this demo to another presenter, show the
saved result,

00:43:18.271 --> 00:43:22.408
reveal the incorrect example, and explain the
missing operational

00:43:22.408 --> 00:43:23.085
stages.

00:43:23.499 --> 00:43:27.034
Keep the native inference command available for
an engineer who

00:43:27.034 --> 00:43:29.492
wants to run new compatible flows.

00:43:29.905 --> 00:43:33.829
Keep the offline replay available for a meeting
with unreliable

00:43:33.842 --> 00:43:34.318
internet.

00:43:34.694 --> 00:43:37.779
Neither route needs a chatbot to classify
traffic.

00:43:38.180 --> 00:43:42.881
Take ten seconds to explain why the eighty four
point four three

00:43:42.881 --> 00:43:46.517
percent score was not enough to trust this
particular prediction,

00:43:46.994 --> 00:43:49.639
even though the score calculation itself was
valid.

00:43:50.000 --> 00:44:00.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Why was the 84.43%
display score insufficient to trust this
prediction?

00:44:00.100 --> 00:44:03.302
The score was uncalibrated and the known label
showed an error.

00:44:03.690 --> 00:44:05.854
Next, choose the setup you actually need.

00:44:06.304 --> 00:44:10.282
For a presentation, use the video, documents,
and recorded

00:44:10.282 --> 00:44:11.095
browser demo.

00:44:11.508 --> 00:44:14.585
For portable evidence checks, use Git and
Python.

00:44:15.023 --> 00:44:19.038
For new neural training or inference, use the
checked Linux CUDA

00:44:19.038 --> 00:44:21.715
environment and separately acquired assets.

00:44:22.115 --> 00:44:24.029
These routes have different requirements.

00:44:24.430 --> 00:44:27.657
A laptop can display the presentation without
supporting the

00:44:27.657 --> 00:44:29.070
model&#x27;s CUDA extensions.

00:44:29.483 --> 00:44:33.373
This separation makes the project usable on
other systems while

00:44:33.386 --> 00:44:36.776
keeping the model execution claims tied to
hardware that was

00:44:36.788 --> 00:44:37.701
actually tested.

00:44:38.167 --> 00:44:41.469
For the portable route, clone the repository,
enter its

00:44:41.469 --> 00:44:43.608
directory, and run the commands shown.

00:44:44.021 --> 00:44:47.435
Use Python three if that is your system&#x27;s
executable name.

00:44:47.848 --> 00:44:51.413
The unit tests and the package and video source
checks use the

00:44:51.413 --> 00:44:54.265
standard library without downloading research
data.

00:44:54.678 --> 00:44:58.856
A successful suite ends with O K, and the
verifiers exit

00:44:58.868 --> 00:44:59.656
successfully.

00:45:00.056 --> 00:45:03.847
These checks protect important behavior such as
input rejection,

00:45:04.259 --> 00:45:07.311
file identity, and consistent published
artifacts.

00:45:07.724 --> 00:45:10.764
They are deliberately different from a GPU
training test.

00:45:11.164 --> 00:45:14.741
Read the workflow record for the exact test
count and commit that

00:45:14.754 --> 00:45:15.329
passed.

00:45:15.800 --> 00:45:18.830
The automated workflow runs the portable checks
on Linux and

00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:23.587
Windows with x eighty six processors, and macOS
on ARM, using

00:45:23.587 --> 00:45:26.479
Python three point ten and three point twelve.

00:45:26.905 --> 00:45:28.520
That makes six combinations.

00:45:28.933 --> 00:45:33.477
Earlier failures exposed text encoding, newline,
and temporary

00:45:33.477 --> 00:45:34.491
directory issues.

00:45:34.892 --> 00:45:38.735
Those were corrected and retained as regression
tests rather than

00:45:38.735 --> 00:45:40.688
removing platforms from the matrix.

00:45:41.076 --> 00:45:44.682
A green workflow means those particular checks
passed on the

00:45:44.682 --> 00:45:45.495
recorded commit.

00:45:45.909 --> 00:45:50.378
It does not establish native Windows or macOS
execution of the

00:45:50.378 --> 00:45:51.542
original neural model.

00:45:51.955 --> 00:45:54.622
The support matrix keeps those boundaries
separate.

00:45:55.100 --> 00:45:58.515
The GPU route uses a specific environment
profile.

00:45:58.928 --> 00:46:02.844
It includes Python three point twelve, Torch two
point nine point

00:46:02.844 --> 00:46:06.835
one with CUDA thirteen, and pinned native
extensions for Mamba

00:46:07.135 --> 00:46:08.286
and causal convolution.

00:46:08.699 --> 00:46:11.939
These extensions contain compiled GPU code.

00:46:12.327 --> 00:46:16.643
A compatible Python package name alone is not
enough: the driver,

00:46:16.844 --> 00:46:21.197
compiler, selected GPU architecture, and
installed binaries must

00:46:21.197 --> 00:46:21.873
work together.

00:46:22.286 --> 00:46:25.238
The current support guide provides the full
commands.

00:46:25.639 --> 00:46:30.205
The generalized builder uses disposable source
copies, checks the

00:46:30.205 --> 00:46:33.758
selected target, and records actual compiler
flags.

00:46:34.146 --> 00:46:36.936
It preserves the original model&#x27;s source
identity.

00:46:37.400 --> 00:46:41.130
After building, run the numerical checker and
the complete model

00:46:41.143 --> 00:46:41.681
checker.

00:46:42.082 --> 00:46:46.050
The numerical checker compares selected GPU
operations with

00:46:46.062 --> 00:46:47.376
reference computations.

00:46:47.777 --> 00:46:51.144
The complete model checker processes six
synthetic fixtures

00:46:51.319 --> 00:46:55.901
through all four blocks, performs an optimizer
update, saves and

00:46:55.901 --> 00:46:59.105
strictly reloads the state, and checks
inference.

00:46:59.506 --> 00:47:03.023
It can bind installed extension hashes to the
completed build

00:47:03.023 --> 00:47:03.536
report.

00:47:03.924 --> 00:47:06.941
This catches failures that a successful import
would miss.

00:47:07.354 --> 00:47:10.997
Synthetic fixtures prove functionality, not
accuracy.

00:47:11.397 --> 00:47:15.052
Only after those gates pass should you acquire
and validate the

00:47:15.052 --> 00:47:18.369
real flow assets and start a new measured
experiment.

00:47:18.833 --> 00:47:22.564
The original model has measured execution on G B
ten.

00:47:22.977 --> 00:47:26.357
A fresh environment on that same hardware also
passed the

00:47:26.370 --> 00:47:28.648
generalized build and model checks.

00:47:29.049 --> 00:47:32.780
Other physical NVIDIA GPUs still need their own
validation.

00:47:33.180 --> 00:47:37.399
There is no complete CPU model fallback, Apple
graphics backend,

00:47:37.850 --> 00:47:39.915
or validated N P U implementation.

00:47:40.316 --> 00:47:44.109
Some reference formulas exist, but normal
imports and active

00:47:44.109 --> 00:47:48.065
execution still depend on CUDA and fused
operations.

00:47:48.466 --> 00:47:52.822
If a setup fails, inspect the fresh build report
and logs.

00:47:53.198 --> 00:47:56.904
Do not turn off checks until it appears green;
determine which

00:47:56.904 --> 00:47:59.733
prerequisite or computation actually failed.

00:48:00.188 --> 00:48:03.493
You can now explain setup without promising that
every machine

00:48:03.505 --> 00:48:04.882
runs every layer.

00:48:05.295 --> 00:48:06.960
Use the simplest route for the job.

00:48:07.373 --> 00:48:09.865
Preserve output directories and their manifests.

00:48:10.265 --> 00:48:13.983
Read failures before retrying with a corrected
configuration.

00:48:14.396 --> 00:48:17.964
When someone asks whether it works elsewhere,
specify whether

00:48:17.977 --> 00:48:22.358
they mean opening the materials, rechecking
evidence, or running

00:48:22.371 --> 00:48:23.122
the neural model.

00:48:23.522 --> 00:48:27.591
Take ten seconds to choose the correct route for
a colleague who

00:48:27.666 --> 00:48:31.034
only needs to present the demo on a Windows
laptop, and the

00:48:31.034 --> 00:48:34.626
separate route for an engineer who wants new GPU
predictions.

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:45.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Which setup is
enough to present, and which setup is needed for
new model predictions?

00:48:45.088 --> 00:48:48.573
A presenter needs the viewing route; new neural
predictions

00:48:48.573 --> 00:48:50.554
require the checked GPU route.

00:48:50.942 --> 00:48:53.224
Now tour the rest of the repository.

00:48:53.638 --> 00:48:57.888
The file guide is the complete inventory, with
one entry for

00:48:58.076 --> 00:48:59.179
every tracked file.

00:48:59.580 --> 00:49:03.053
This chapter explains how the areas fit together
so that the

00:49:03.066 --> 00:49:07.880
inventory is useful instead of becoming a wall
of unfamiliar

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:08.444
names.

00:49:08.845 --> 00:49:10.450
Tracked means included in Git.

00:49:10.851 --> 00:49:15.502
Locally downloaded source, raw data,
checkpoints, environments,

00:49:15.678 --> 00:49:17.295
and temporary runs are separate.

00:49:17.684 --> 00:49:21.156
A GitHub release attachment is another separate
object: it

00:49:21.156 --> 00:49:24.692
belongs to its tagged release, not automatically
to the current

00:49:24.692 --> 00:49:25.431
working tree.

00:49:25.900 --> 00:49:29.309
At the root, the read me is the front door and
the four Python

00:49:29.322 --> 00:49:31.139
entry points coordinate execution.

00:49:31.553 --> 00:49:35.501
Configs contains the source based fine tuning
recipe, the short

00:49:35.501 --> 00:49:38.271
pretraining recipe, and the asset inventory.

00:49:38.672 --> 00:49:42.081
Requirements separates packages used for
different activities,

00:49:42.319 --> 00:49:46.443
such as GPU execution, browser checks, and video
production.

00:49:46.856 --> 00:49:51.506
Dot git ignore keeps local outputs and sensitive
settings out of

00:49:51.506 --> 00:49:52.308
normal tracking.

00:49:52.709 --> 00:49:56.407
Dot git attributes preserves the exact bytes of
recorded

00:49:56.407 --> 00:49:59.716
documents and configurations across platforms.

00:50:00.104 --> 00:50:04.015
Neither file establishes data rights or
scientific correctness;

00:50:04.416 --> 00:50:06.797
they support reliable repository handling.

00:50:07.267 --> 00:50:10.815
The tools directory contains programs that
support the

00:50:10.827 --> 00:50:11.530
experiment.

00:50:11.981 --> 00:50:16.870
Fetch assets obtains verified external files,
while profile C S

00:50:16.983 --> 00:50:18.914
Vs scans the uploaded data.

00:50:19.315 --> 00:50:23.164
The CUDA builders compile pinned extensions;
operator and

00:50:23.164 --> 00:50:25.734
complete model checks test their behavior.

00:50:26.148 --> 00:50:30.536
Benchmark measures model timing, and probe
export records whether

00:50:30.536 --> 00:50:32.567
a computation graph can be captured.

00:50:32.981 --> 00:50:37.043
The new calibration command reads validation
flows and writes a

00:50:37.055 --> 00:50:40.026
checkpoint bound temperature file with fit
evidence.

00:50:40.428 --> 00:50:44.302
Publish evidence copies reviewed records into
the package; its

00:50:44.302 --> 00:50:48.426
name does not mean arbitrary files are
automatically sent to

00:50:48.439 --> 00:50:48.978
GitHub.

00:50:49.392 --> 00:50:53.015
The file guide explains every tool&#x27;s inputs,
outputs, and

00:50:53.015 --> 00:50:53.679
purpose.

00:50:54.121 --> 00:50:57.341
Other tools build the customer package, the
plain language

00:50:57.341 --> 00:51:00.938
learning guide, replay outputs, and teaching
materials.

00:51:01.339 --> 00:51:05.211
The video pipeline separates the lesson source,
speech rendering,

00:51:05.436 --> 00:51:07.842
visual assembly, and browser page.

00:51:08.256 --> 00:51:11.313
Codex authors the narration and explanations.

00:51:11.714 --> 00:51:15.398
Microsoft&#x27;s Andrew neural voice converts that
text into sound;

00:51:15.862 --> 00:51:18.807
the speech service does not research or write
the lesson.

00:51:19.220 --> 00:51:23.468
Verification tools compare identities and
inspect encoded media.

00:51:23.882 --> 00:51:27.353
The archived interactive course builders remain
for reference

00:51:27.365 --> 00:51:31.337
continuity, but the retired public course route
is excluded from

00:51:31.337 --> 00:51:31.914
deployment.

00:51:32.315 --> 00:51:34.996
The one active video is the current teaching
path.

00:51:35.467 --> 00:51:39.339
Tests contains controlled cases for the
wrappers, acquisition,

00:51:39.740 --> 00:51:43.161
portability, runtime checking, and presentation
tooling.

00:51:43.587 --> 00:51:47.886
The workflow files run portable checks and
publish the selected

00:51:47.886 --> 00:51:48.801
browser material.

00:51:49.214 --> 00:51:52.961
Docs contains foundational explanations and the
customer package.

00:51:53.412 --> 00:51:57.485
Figures are reusable charts; evidence contains
original

00:51:57.485 --> 00:52:01.671
experiment records; audit evidence contains
later rechecks.

00:52:02.072 --> 00:52:05.518
Research records preserve plans and council
outcomes.

00:52:05.919 --> 00:52:08.989
A council discussion can improve a design, but
it is not an

00:52:09.102 --> 00:52:10.744
executed GPU test.

00:52:11.145 --> 00:52:15.493
An unratified review remains unratified even
when ordinary

00:52:15.493 --> 00:52:18.263
implementation later fixes a technical problem.

00:52:18.664 --> 00:52:21.959
Read the status and evidence scope of each
record.

00:52:22.434 --> 00:52:26.834
The original customer release contains a
briefing PDF, editable

00:52:26.847 --> 00:52:31.148
PowerPoint, slide PDF, offline package, and
supporting

00:52:31.160 --> 00:52:33.618
presentation materials at its recorded revision.

00:52:34.031 --> 00:52:36.464
It remains a frozen experiment package.

00:52:36.877 --> 00:52:40.902
The new course has its own versioned video,
transcript, captions,

00:52:41.115 --> 00:52:42.432
and companion materials.

00:52:42.821 --> 00:52:45.077
A transcript is the narration in text.

00:52:45.479 --> 00:52:48.563
Caption files add time boundaries for video
players.

00:52:48.964 --> 00:52:53.265
Chapter metadata supports navigation, and the
media manifest

00:52:53.277 --> 00:52:55.396
records hashes and provenance.

00:52:55.810 --> 00:52:59.245
Use the current support matrix for later
portability work.

00:52:59.659 --> 00:53:03.671
Do not assume the original release ZIP silently
changes whenever

00:53:03.671 --> 00:53:05.903
the repository gains a new file.

00:53:06.405 --> 00:53:10.089
For a new colleague, begin with the read me and
this course, then

00:53:10.089 --> 00:53:13.096
use the file guide to explore a specific task.

00:53:13.497 --> 00:53:17.107
Before running commands, read the current
support matrix and the

00:53:17.107 --> 00:53:17.645
runbook.

00:53:18.059 --> 00:53:21.204
Before presenting a result, trace it from the
results document to

00:53:21.217 --> 00:53:24.275
its manifest, log, and predictions.

00:53:24.663 --> 00:53:27.608
You do not need to memorize every audit JSON
file.

00:53:28.022 --> 00:53:30.403
You need to know which question it answers and
how it was

00:53:30.403 --> 00:53:31.004
produced.

00:53:31.418 --> 00:53:34.839
Take ten seconds to choose the file you would
open to check a

00:53:34.839 --> 00:53:38.874
platform claim, and the record you would open to
verify an actual

00:53:38.874 --> 00:53:39.626
training result.

00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:50.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Where would you
verify a platform claim and an actual training
result?

00:53:50.100 --> 00:53:53.806
Use the support matrix for platform claims and
the run records

00:53:53.806 --> 00:53:54.895
for training evidence.

00:53:55.371 --> 00:53:57.337
Finally, consider hardware deployment.

00:53:57.738 --> 00:54:01.719
The model only benchmark measured a median batch
one latency of

00:54:01.732 --> 00:54:05.300
about zero point eight eight milliseconds on G B
ten.

00:54:05.701 --> 00:54:09.056
It used twenty warmup iterations and one hundred
measured

00:54:09.056 --> 00:54:09.882
repetitions.

00:54:10.271 --> 00:54:14.227
That timing excludes packet capture, waiting for
enough packets,

00:54:14.390 --> 00:54:18.346
preprocessing, transfers, model loading, and
alert handling.

00:54:18.734 --> 00:54:21.764
A fast classifier can still be part of a slow
pipeline.

00:54:22.140 --> 00:54:26.134
End to end latency must be measured across the
complete path a

00:54:26.147 --> 00:54:28.000
real observation follows.

00:54:28.442 --> 00:54:32.160
A network interface card moves traffic into and
out of a machine.

00:54:32.561 --> 00:54:35.804
A Smart N I C can take on additional packet
handling or

00:54:35.804 --> 00:54:36.567
computation.

00:54:36.968 --> 00:54:41.400
An N P U accelerates neural operations that its
runtime supports.

00:54:41.801 --> 00:54:45.557
A possible future division places capture and
some flow handling

00:54:45.569 --> 00:54:48.800
near the network interface, with a host
coordinating buffers,

00:54:49.037 --> 00:54:51.892
features, inference, and alert policy.

00:54:52.293 --> 00:54:54.020
This is an architectural proposal.

00:54:54.421 --> 00:54:59.179
Our repository has not executed Net Mamba Plus
on a Smart N I C

00:54:59.304 --> 00:55:03.523
or N P U, and it does not establish a direct
memory transfer path

00:55:03.523 --> 00:55:04.462
on the measured hardware.

00:55:04.908 --> 00:55:09.089
A model only checkpoint export saves parameter
tensors without

00:55:09.101 --> 00:55:10.428
the training optimizer.

00:55:10.816 --> 00:55:14.734
A graph export tries to represent the
computation for another

00:55:14.746 --> 00:55:15.785
execution system.

00:55:16.198 --> 00:55:17.437
These are different operations.

00:55:17.838 --> 00:55:21.255
Our strict graph capture probe failed at the
custom causal

00:55:21.255 --> 00:55:25.798
convolution CUDA operator, while ordinary eager
GPU inference

00:55:25.798 --> 00:55:26.299
works.

00:55:26.712 --> 00:55:30.366
Copying the weight file to an N P U therefore
does not solve

00:55:30.366 --> 00:55:30.942
deployment.

00:55:31.343 --> 00:55:35.122
A port needs supported operators or equivalent
implementations,

00:55:35.335 --> 00:55:38.602
compatible layouts, controlled precision, and
numerical

00:55:38.614 --> 00:55:40.492
validation against the known outputs.

00:55:40.892 --> 00:55:44.672
The recorded failure is useful because it
identifies a concrete

00:55:44.772 --> 00:55:47.864
obstacle rather than a vague future
optimization.

00:55:48.334 --> 00:55:51.965
After implementing a target backend, first
compare its full model

00:55:51.965 --> 00:55:53.568
outputs with the checked reference.

00:55:53.969 --> 00:55:57.487
Where training is part of the target, compare
gradients as well.

00:55:57.901 --> 00:56:01.758
If reducing numerical precision through
quantization, measure the

00:56:01.758 --> 00:56:06.078
effect on the labeled task instead of assuming
the same accuracy.

00:56:06.466 --> 00:56:10.987
Then measure throughput, tail latency, memory,
buffering, and

00:56:10.987 --> 00:56:13.554
packet drops under an actual traffic path.

00:56:13.942 --> 00:56:18.350
A compiler success message proves a build stage,
not useful

00:56:18.362 --> 00:56:19.414
security behavior.

00:56:19.815 --> 00:56:23.421
Only a complete measured pipeline can support a
deployment claim,

00:56:23.872 --> 00:56:27.504
and none of those N P U or Smart N I C
measurements exists

00:56:27.504 --> 00:56:28.067
here yet.

00:56:28.521 --> 00:56:32.126
The new calibration experiment improves the
project&#x27;s confidence

00:56:32.126 --> 00:56:35.618
handling, but it does not close the main
research and deployment

00:56:35.618 --> 00:56:36.144
gaps.

00:56:36.532 --> 00:56:40.212
We have not matched the paper accuracy, repeated
its full

00:56:40.212 --> 00:56:43.667
pretraining, or established live capture and
extraction.

00:56:44.068 --> 00:56:48.161
Calibration was fitted using data already
involved in checkpoint

00:56:48.161 --> 00:56:50.827
selection, and the test set was already seen.

00:56:51.241 --> 00:56:55.184
Its behavior on independent customer traffic
remains unknown.

00:56:55.572 --> 00:57:00.003
Unknown attack detection, operational alert
guarantees, and an N

00:57:00.015 --> 00:57:01.831
P U deployment are still missing.

00:57:02.219 --> 00:57:06.087
Other physical GPUs need their own execution
checks.

00:57:06.500 --> 00:57:10.593
These limits stay beside the positive evidence,
including the

00:57:10.593 --> 00:57:12.420
results that did not improve.

00:57:12.900 --> 00:57:15.691
Now explain the project aloud in your own words.

00:57:16.092 --> 00:57:19.634
Start with the paper&#x27;s idea, distinguish the
uploaded packet

00:57:19.634 --> 00:57:23.439
files from the training flows, and follow inputs
through training

00:57:23.602 --> 00:57:24.403
and inference.

00:57:24.816 --> 00:57:27.319
Give the measured result and one real error.

00:57:27.695 --> 00:57:30.961
Explain what the repository adds and which setup
your listener

00:57:30.974 --> 00:57:31.500
needs.

00:57:31.938 --> 00:57:33.865
Finish with the remaining deployment work.

00:57:34.278 --> 00:57:37.232
You have ninety seconds, and you can pause
longer.

00:57:37.645 --> 00:57:41.788
The transcript, slides, and every file guide
remain available

00:57:41.788 --> 00:57:42.289
afterward.

00:57:42.702 --> 00:57:46.857
Your strongest presentation is one in which each
claim leads to

00:57:46.857 --> 00:57:51.100
evidence and each limitation leads to a clear
next test.

00:57:51.467 --> 00:59:21.467
[Practice pause: 90 seconds] Explain the data
choice, tested result, repository and remaining
deployment work.

00:59:21.579 --> 00:59:22.994
Here is a concise handoff.

00:59:23.382 --> 00:59:26.888
We built a checked execution and evidence layer
around the

00:59:26.900 --> 00:59:30.068
authors&#x27; original Net Mamba Plus model.

00:59:30.468 --> 00:59:34.775
We profiled the two uploaded packet CSVs and
established why they

00:59:34.788 --> 00:59:36.728
were not compatible flow inputs.

00:59:37.117 --> 00:59:40.998
Using the authors&#x27; native flow data, three full
training runs

00:59:41.110 --> 00:59:44.954
averaged eighty six point six five percent test
accuracy.

00:59:45.355 --> 00:59:49.874
The repository includes strict inference,
independent results, a

00:59:49.975 --> 00:59:53.543
recorded demo, setup checks, and teaching
materials.

00:59:53.956 --> 00:59:57.537
The paper number and a live deployed intrusion
detection system

00:59:57.724 --> 00:59:59.627
remain unproven by this work.
